PSALM LXII  



  David, in imminent danger, flees to God for help and safety, 
   1, 2; 
  points out the designs of his adversaries, 3, 4; 
  encourages his soul to wait on God, 5-8; 
  shows the vanity of trusting in man, and of trusting in riches, 
   9, 10; 
  and concludes with asserting that power and mercy belong to 
   God, and that he will give to every man according to his works, 
   11, 12.

 NOTES ON PSALM LXII 

The title, "To the chief Musician, to Jeduthun," may mean that the Psalm was sent to him who was the chief or leader of the band of the family of Jeduthun. It appears that Asaph, Jeduthun, and Heman, were chief singers in the time of David; that they, with their families, presided over different departments of the vocal and instrumental worship in the tabernacle,
